 ## Overview
-Tiled 2D Gaussian Splatting Rasterization is a GPU-accelerated, differentiable rendering method that projects 3D Gaussian primitives into screen space and composites them into a 2D image using tile-based parallel processing [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of rendering millions of splats by partitioning the image into fixed-size macro-tiles, sorting Gaussians by depth within each tile, and using CUDA thread blocks to cooperatively accumulate pixel colors via alpha blending [S1, S8, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its parent [3D Gaussian Splatting Rendering (CPU-Sorted, Instanced)](2058-3d-gaussian-splatting-rendering-cpu-sorted-instanced.md) by moving the sorting and blending logic entirely to the GPU for real-time performance, and from [Gaussian Splat Rendering (Screen-Space Quad)](2075-gaussian-splat-rendering-screen-space-quad.md) by avoiding fixed-function rasterizer overdraw through explicit software-style alpha blending in shaders [S1, unofficial][S12].
+Tiled 2D Gaussian Splatting Rasterization is a GPU-accelerated, differentiable rendering method for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that partitions the image plane into macro-tiles to efficiently composite millions of 2D Gaussian splats [S1, S2, S8, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of per-pixel sorting and redundant evaluation by assigning a CUDA thread block to each tile, where threads cooperatively fetch pre-sorted Gaussians and perform iterative alpha blending [S1, S7, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its parent [3D Gaussian Splatting Rendering (CPU-Sorted, Instanced)](2058-3d-gaussian-splatting-rendering-cpu-sorted-instanced.md) by moving the sorting and blending logic entirely to the GPU for real-time performance, and from [Gaussian Splat Rendering (Screen-Space Quad)](2075-gaussian-splat-rendering-screen-space-quad.md) by using explicit tile-based memory management and shared memory synchronization rather than standard graphics pipeline blending [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
